  <!-- ============ SECTION 1 ============ -->
  
  <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="card ci">
      <div class="callout suc">THE MOST PRACTICALLY-ENCOUNTERED PROVISION IN THIS ENTIRE CLUSTER: unlike §331–334, which target specific sophisticated failures (deliberate falsification, auditor misconduct, audit committee corruption), §335 is what actually bites ORDINARY companies simply late in filing their accounts — flagged repeatedly across this series' earlier posters on retention (§257–263) and disclosure (§§325–329), now covered in full.</div>

  
      <div class="ctitle ci">Where §325's disclosure obligation gets genuine teeth</div>
      <div class="principle">§325 required disclosure; §329 required a formal completeness check; §335 supplies what actually HAPPENS when a company fails to comply — a structured, escalating coercive-fine procedure administered by the Bundesamt für Justiz. Given how many German Kapitalgesellschaften are small, ordinary businesses rather than sophisticated public-market issuers, this is likely the single MOST frequently applied enforcement provision this entire Straf-/Bußgeld-/Ordnungsgeld Subsection contains.</div>
      <div class="elabel">HOW §335 MAPS TO GERMAN ACCOUNTING PRINCIPLES</div>
      <div class="check-list">
        <span><strong>Proportionality, once again</strong> — the size-sensitive fine reduction for late-but-eventual compliance continues the SAME cost-benefit theme running throughout this entire poster series</span>
        <span><strong>Compliance-forcing, not merely punitive</strong> — the escalating "repeat the order, threaten a further fine" mechanism is designed to genuinely COMPEL disclosure, not simply punish its absence once and move on</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cs">
      <div class="ctitle cs">What §335 covers, at a glance</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-06-17"><span>§</span><span>Core content</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-06-17"><span>335(1)</span><span>The basic Ordnungsgeldverfahren mechanism, against representative body members and the company itself</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-06-17"><span>335(1d)</span><span>Mandatory BaFin notification for capital-market-oriented companies</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-06-17"><span>335(4)</span><span>The six-week warning period; escalating, repeating fines; size-sensitive late-compliance reductions</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-06-17"><span>335(6)</span><span>A tool to request size-classification data directly from the company</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 2 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope — Representative Body Members AND the Company Itself</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">A dual target, unlike most other provisions in this cluster</div>
      <div class="principle">§335 proceedings can be directed against the members of the Vertretungsorgan (representative body — the management board or equivalent) personally, and SEPARATELY, the SAME proceeding may ALSO be conducted directly against the Kapitalgesellschaft itself — a genuinely dual-track approach not shared by every other provision this cluster has covered.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 3 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>§335(1) — the Basic Mechanism</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">A structured, multi-stage coercive-fine proceeding</div>
      <div class="principle">§335(1) provides that where representative body members fail to comply with the §325/§325a disclosure obligations established earlier in this series, the Bundesamt für Justiz conducts an Ordnungsgeldverfahren under §335(2)-(6) — a structured proceeding, not a single automatic fine, involving formal warning, an opportunity to object, and — where non-compliance persists — actual imposition and escalating repetition.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 4 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>"No Escape via Your Own Earlier Non-Compliance"</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">You cannot use your own prior failure to shield yourself from this one</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">§335(1) sentence 3 provides that the proceeding is NOT blocked merely because an EARLIER, PREREQUISITE obligation — such as actually PREPARING the Jahresabschluss/Konzernabschluss in the first place, or promptly engaging an auditor — has itself not yet been fulfilled. A company cannot escape or delay the disclosure-enforcement proceeding by pointing to its own even-more-fundamental failure to have prepared the underlying documents at all.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 5 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Worked Example — a Company That Never Even Prepared Its Accounts</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">Compounding failures don't compound excuses</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 1: A company that missed both preparation and disclosure deadlines</div>
        <div class="wk-body">A medium-sized GmbH has failed not only to disclose its Jahresabschluss within the §325(1a) twelve-month deadline, but has never even PREPARED it in the first place, and has not engaged an auditor at all.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">The Bundesamt für Justiz may STILL open an Ordnungsgeldverfahren under §335(1) for the disclosure failure specifically — the company cannot argue "we couldn't disclose because we never prepared the accounts" as a defence; §335(1) sentence 3 explicitly forecloses that argument, ensuring a company cannot escape enforcement simply by failing even more comprehensively.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 6 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>§335(1d) — the BaFin Notification Loop for Listed Companies</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">A genuine regulatory feedback loop, not merely an isolated enforcement process</div>
      <div class="principle">§335(1d) requires the Bundesamt to notify BaFin (the Bundesanstalt für Finanzdienstleistungsaufsicht, Germany's financial regulator) IMMEDIATELY whenever an Ordnungsgeld is imposed against a capital-market-oriented company (per §264d) or a member of its representative body — and to further notify BaFin if an appeal (Beschwerde) is lodged against the fine, and of that appeal's ultimate outcome. This creates a genuine cross-regulatory information channel: a listed company's disclosure failure does not stay confined to the Bundesamt's own files, but reaches the securities regulator directly and automatically.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 7 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Worked Example — a Listed Company's Failure Reaching the Securities Regulator</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">An administrative fine that isn't just a quiet, isolated matter</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 2: A listed AG's fine automatically reaching BaFin</div>
        <div class="wk-body">A capital-market-oriented AG, established earlier in this series, misses its shortened §325(4) four-month disclosure deadline, and the Bundesamt imposes an Ordnungsgeld against the company.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">The Bundesamt AUTOMATICALLY notifies BaFin of this fine under §335(1d) — the AG's disclosure failure becomes visible to the SECURITIES regulator, not merely to the Bundesamt's own internal process, reflecting the genuinely heightened public-market significance of a LISTED company's disclosure compliance relative to an ordinary private company's.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 8 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>§335(4) — the Six-Week Warning, and the Escalating Repeat-Fine Structure</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">A genuine second chance, followed by an ongoing, repeating compliance mechanism</div>
      <div class="principle">§335(4) sentence 1 provides that if the parties do NOT comply with the legal obligation within SIX WEEKS of receiving the formal Androhung (warning notice), or do not justify their non-compliance via a formal Einspruch (objection), the Ordnungsgeld is IMPOSED — and SIMULTANEOUSLY, the earlier order is REPEATED, with the THREAT of a FURTHER, renewed Ordnungsgeld if compliance still doesn't follow. This is a genuinely ESCALATING, ongoing mechanism designed to keep pressuring for actual compliance, rather than a single one-time punitive event.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 9 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Worked Example — Walking Through the Full Timeline</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">A multi-stage process, not a single automatic penalty</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 3: A late-filing medium-sized GmbH's complete Ordnungsgeld journey</div>
        <div class="wk-body">A medium-sized GmbH misses its ordinary 12-month filing deadline. The Bundesamt sends a formal Androhung, giving the company six weeks to comply or object. The company does neither within that period.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">The Bundesamt IMPOSES the threatened Ordnungsgeld (a MINIMUM of €2,500, up to €25,000) under §335(4) sentence 1 — and SIMULTANEOUSLY repeats its original disclosure order, with a THREAT of a FURTHER Ordnungsgeld if the company still hasn't complied. If the company continues to ignore the requirement, this cycle can genuinely REPEAT — each round imposing a further fine and renewing the threat, until compliance is finally achieved.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 10 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>The Size-Sensitive Reduction for Late-But-Eventual Compliance</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">Rewarding genuine, if tardy, compliance — and scaling the reward by company size</div>
      <div class="principle">§335(4) sentence 2 provides that WHERE the company DOES fulfil the disclosure obligation, but only AFTER the six-week deadline has passed, the Bundesamt must REDUCE the fine, scaled by company size:</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th"><span>Company category</span><span>Reduced fine</span></div>
        <div class="tr"><span>Micro-corporation using the §326(2) Hinterlegung option</span><span>€500</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt"><span>Small corporation (§267(1))</span><span>€1,000</span></div>
        <div class="tr"><span>Any other company where a higher fine was threatened</span><span>€2,500 (the statutory minimum)</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="note">A genuinely elegant, size-sensitive mitigation mechanism, continuing the SAME cost-benefit relief theme this poster series has documented since §267's own size thresholds — smaller companies that ultimately comply, even late, are treated proportionately more leniently than the ordinary escalated fine would otherwise impose.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 11 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Worked Example — Contrasting the Reduced Fines Across Company Sizes</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">The exact same lateness, three genuinely different outcomes</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 4: Three companies, all filing exactly two weeks late, past the six-week deadline</div>
        <div class="wk-body">A micro-corporation using Hinterlegung, a small corporation, and a large corporation each file their accounts two weeks after their own respective six-week Androhung deadline expired — genuinely, actually complying, just later than they should have.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">The micro-corporation's fine is reduced to just <strong>€500</strong>. The small corporation's fine is reduced to <strong>€1,000</strong>. The large corporation, not qualifying for either specific reduction, faces the STATUTORY MINIMUM of <strong>€2,500</strong> instead — the SAME two-week lateness producing a fivefold difference in outcome purely based on the company's own size classification.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 12 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>§335(6) — Requesting Size-Classification Data Directly</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">A practical tool for the Bundesamt to classify companies it lacks information about</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">§335(6) permits the Bundesamt, where it lacks sufficient information to properly classify a company's own §267/§267a size category, to REQUIRE the company itself to provide the specific data needed — the balance sheet total (net of any negative equity per §268(3)), revenue (§277(1)), and average employee count (§267(5)) — for both the relevant year and any earlier years needed for the classification test. A genuinely practical administrative tool, ensuring the size-sensitive relief mechanisms this poster series has documented throughout can actually be APPLIED correctly, even where the Bundesamt doesn't already independently know a company's precise size.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 13 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>§335(2) — Borrowed Procedural Mechanics</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Reusing established German procedural frameworks rather than reinventing them</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">§335(2) applies specific provisions of the OWiG (Gesetz über Ordnungswidrigkeiten, Germany's general administrative-offences procedural code) and the FamFG (the procedural code otherwise governing family-law and voluntary-jurisdiction matters) correspondingly to the §335 Ordnungsgeldverfahren — a structural choice this poster series has now seen repeatedly (§298(1)'s own "corresponding application" borrowing of individual-accounts rules for group accounts is a conceptually similar pattern): rather than legislating an entirely bespoke procedure, HGB borrows established procedural mechanics from elsewhere in German law wherever genuinely suitable.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 14 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Practical Difficulties</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2">
      <div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div>
          <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div>
          <div class="dot-list">
            <span>Whether a formal Einspruch (objection) genuinely justifies the non-compliance, versus being a procedurally valid but substantively insufficient excuse</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="elabel">PRACTICAL DIFFICULTIES</div>
          <div class="dot-list">
            <span>Managing the genuinely escalating, repeating nature of the fine mechanism for a company that remains persistently non-compliant across multiple rounds</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 15 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Relationship to IFRS</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2">
      <div class="ctitle ci">Disclosure enforcement sits entirely outside any accounting standard</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">Precisely as with the earlier disclosure-filing poster's own observation, IFRS itself has no equivalent Ordnungsgeld-style mechanism — HOW a jurisdiction actually COMPELS compliance with its own disclosure requirements is left entirely to local law, wherever IFRS is adopted. §335 completes this poster series' documentation of the practical enforcement layer sitting beneath every disclosure obligation this series has covered — the genuine mechanism ensuring §325's requirements are not merely aspirational.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 16 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s16"><span class="num">16</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">Where every §335 event lands</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-16-1-14"><span>Event / obligation</span><span>Document affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Ordnungsgeld imposed</span><span>—</span><span>A financial penalty, not itself affecting any specific statement's content</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-16-1-14"><span>BaFin notification (§335(1d))</span><span>—</span><span>A regulatory information flow, cross-referencing the company's own capital-market-oriented status</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 17 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s17"><span class="num">17</span><h2>Related Provisions — Cross-Reference Map</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">How §335 connects across HGB Book 3</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-08-2"><span>Provision</span><span>Nature of the connection</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>§325, §325a</span><span>The disclosure obligations §335 exists to enforce</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>§329</span><span>The completeness check that identifies deficiencies §335's own proceeding then addresses</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>§267, §267a, §326</span><span>Supplies the size classifications underlying §335(4)'s own late-compliance fine reductions</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>§264d</span><span>Triggers the §335(1d) BaFin notification obligation for capital-market-oriented companies</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 18 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s18"><span class="num">18</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="three-col">
    <div class="card cn">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Ordnungsgeldverfahren</div><div class="glossary-def">Coercive fine proceeding — the structured, multi-stage process this provision establishes for enforcing disclosure compliance.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Androhung</div><div class="glossary-def">The formal warning notice giving the company six weeks to comply or object before a fine is actually imposed.</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="card cn">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Einspruch</div><div class="glossary-def">A formal objection that may justify non-compliance within the six-week window.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">BaFin</div><div class="glossary-def">Bundesanstalt für Finanzdienstleistungsaufsicht — Germany's financial/securities regulator, automatically notified of fines against capital-market-oriented companies.</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="card cn">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Vertretungsorgan</div><div class="glossary-def">Representative body — the management board or equivalent, one of two possible §335 proceeding targets alongside the company itself.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Wiedereinsetzung</div><div class="glossary-def">Reinstatement of a missed procedural deadline where the company was blamelessly prevented from acting in time.</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  </div>
</StandardPageLayout>
